Virtual environments:
Create work environments that are isolated from the rest of your computer. Allows independent usage of software and applications. Prevents conflicts for different projects needing different versions of a packages. Keeps the project safe from updates done on the whole computer. Only packages downloaded into the environment are available, none from the 'python base'.
How to set one up:
- Use venv, 
- Creates a Scripts on windows, a subdirectory with a copy of the python executable
- Also a Lib\site-packages is created, another directory
- Install packages using pip/ conda install directly into environment
- Contain in directory, not movable/copyable, can recreate same environment again
- No project code in the environment


UltraNest:
Bayesian inference and nested sampling (Monte Carlo technique) package, very robust. 
Install using pip or conda
Nested Sampling - iterates through random live points to find the live point with the lowest likelihood, eliminates it
and replace with a point of higher likelihood. Repeated until the live points concentrate near the best fitting regions.
Each cycle makes a new region of parameter space within the old so the shells are nested inside the previous region. 
Evidence is the average likelihood over the whole prior volume, which shrinks as nested sampling works through the 
iterations.
Bayesian stats - P(θ∣D)=P(D∣θ)P(θ)​/P(D)

P(θ∣D) = posterior
P(D∣θ) = likelihood
P(θ) = prior
P(D) = evidence

SSH:
Enables connection of two computers over a network, allows control of remote Linux machine through own terminal.
PuTTy - program for windows that uses SSH, use it to create an SSH connection. Enter in Hostname, Username and port.
VS Code - use SSH in more powerful way, connects to remote machine using SSH, stores files on remote machine and editor
is locally on own PC. Don't have to keep copying files back and forth. 
How to set up VS Code - download and install, install remote SSH extension from Microsoft, check SSH works first, open
SSH config file (Ctrl + Shift + P) then type "Remote-SSH: Open SSH Configuration File", choose 
e.g "C:\Users\YourName\.ssh\config", add server with nickname and username, then press (Ctrl + Shift + P) and 
run "Remote-SSH: Connect to Host", click on nickname, will see SSH: nickname in corner, open project folder, 
open remote terminal, set up python environment and select python interpreter

Luminosity:
We can measure apparent brightness and apply bolometric corrections to get total flux reaching Earth, and can determine distance and
then use the luminosity equation L=4π(d^2)Fbol​.

Surface Metallicity:
Metallicity measures abundance of heavy elements over hydrogen strongly affects opacity, luminosity, temperature and 
evolutionary timescale. Measured using spectroscopy and identifying the iron absorption lines and compare to stellar models.

Effective Temperature:
Depicts how atoms are ionised, which energy states they occupy. Measured by interferometry, obtaining an angular diameter
and total flux received on Earth then Teff​=(4Fbol​​/σθ^2)^(1/4), Fbol = bolometric flux and θ = angular diameter. Could
also use spectroscopy.

Upper and Lower turning points:
Where wave is no longer propagating, it is being reflected.
Upper turning point close to surface.
Wave propagation - for p-mode, propagates is mode freq > Lamb freq and acoustic cutoff freq, reflects is one not met
Upper - when mode freq = acoustic freq, high freq have turning points near photosphere
Lower - when mode freq = Lamb freq, as mode deg l increases, refraction happens earlier before reaching core

Marginalisation related to the corner plots:
Integrating over unwanted parameters to get posterior distribution with only wanted parameters.
Can look at a two parameters probability distribution after all remaining parameters have been marginalised so measurement
includes uncertainty from all parameters in 2D marginalised posteriors.

